# MindleapV1: University Mental Health Platform Architecture

## 1. System Architecture

The architecture relies on a modern, asynchronous FastAPI backend suited for high concurrency and real-time features.

```mermaid
graph TD
    %% Clients
    Browser[Web Browser / React] -->|HTTPS / WSS| Nginx[API Gateway / Nginx / Traefik]
    MobileApp[Mobile App / React Native] -->|HTTPS / WSS| Nginx
    
    %% Gateway
    Nginx --> FastAPI_App[FastAPI Core Application]
    
    %% Core Services / Modules
    subgraph FastAPI Monolithic Micro-Modules
        Auth[Auth & Identity Module]
        Chatbot[AI Chatbot & WebSocket Module]
        Appointments[Scheduling & Counseling Module]
        Analytics[Telemetry & Analytics Module]
        Resources[Content & Resources Module]
    end
    
    FastAPI_App --> Auth
    FastAPI_App --> Chatbot
    FastAPI_App --> Appointments
    FastAPI_App --> Analytics
    FastAPI_App --> Resources
    
    %% Databases & Integrations
    Auth --> PostgreSQL[(PostgreSQL - Relational DB)]
    Appointments --> PostgreSQL
    Resources --> PostgreSQL
    
    Analytics --> ClickHouse[(ClickHouse / TimescaleDB - Analytics)]
    
    %% Cache, Message Broker, and Background Workers
    Chatbot --> Redis[(Redis Cache & Session Broker)]
    Appointments --> Redis
    
    Redis -.-> |Task Queues| CeleryWorkers[Celery Workers]
    
    CeleryWorkers --> |Async Processing| PostgreSQL
    CeleryWorkers --> |Email Notifications| SMTP[SMTP / SendGrid]
    
    %% External Services
    Chatbot --> |LLM Inference API| OpenAILLM[External LLM Provider - OpenAI / Local LLaMA]
```

---

## 2. Production Project Structure

A domain-driven design (DDD) structure is used to maintain separation of concerns, allowing specific modules to be broken out into microservices if needed later.

```text
mindleap_backend/
├── alembic/                    # Database migrations
├── app/
│   ├── main.py                 # FastAPI application factory and entry point
│   ├── core/                   # Application-wide settings and configurations
│   │   ├── config.py           # Pydantic BaseSettings for env vars
│   │   ├── security.py         # JWT, password hashing
│   │   ├── exceptions.py       # Global exception handlers
│   │   └── celery_app.py       # Celery application initialization
│   ├── api/                    # API Routing
│   │   ├── dependencies.py     # Reusable dependencies (get_db, current_user)
│   │   └── v1/
│   │       ├── api_router.py   # FastAPI APIRouter aggregator
│   │       └── ...
│   ├── modules/                # Domain specific business logic (Micro-modules)
│   │   ├── auth/               # Authentication and identity
│   │   │   ├── models.py       # SQLAlchemy ORM models
│   │   │   ├── schemas.py      # Pydantic validation schemas
│   │   │   ├── services.py     # Business logic
│   │   │   ├── router.py       # FastAPI endpoints
│   │   │   └── tasks.py        # Celery background tasks (e.g., welcome email)
│   │   ├── chatbot/            # GenAI chat and conversation memory
│   │   ├── appointments/       # Counselor scheduling
│   │   ├── analytics/          # Platform telemetry
│   │   └── resources/          # Mental health content delivery
│   ├── db/                     # Global Database utilities
│   │   ├── session.py          # SQLAlchemy async engine Setup
│   │   └── base.py             # Declarative base
│   └── websockets/             # WebSocket connection managers
│       └── connection_manager.py
├── tests/                      # Pytest suite (unit and integration)
├── scripts/                    # Helper scripts (db setup, seed data)
├── docker-compose.yml          # Local development stack
├── docker-compose.prod.yml     # Production scaling stack
├── Dockerfile                  # Multi-stage Dockerfile for FastAPI
├── Dockerfile.celery           # Dockerfile for Celery worker
├── requirements.txt            # Poetry or pip dependencies
└── README.md
```

---

## 3. Microservice Module Explanation

While initially deployed as a modular monolith for operational simplicity, the codebase is structured around independent "Micro-Modules" that can be easily extracted into separate microservices.

*   **Auth & Identity Module:** Handles JWT-based authentication, user signup/login, password resets, and role-based access control (RBAC) distinguishing between Students, Counselors, and Admins.
*   **Chatbot Module:** Manages interaction with the LLM (e.g., OpenAI, Langchain). It encapsulates conversation history state, prompt engineering specific to mental health (ensuring empathetic, safe boundaries), and WebSocket streaming logic.
*   **Appointments (Scheduling) Module:** Manages the calendar system, booking counselor appointments, and managing availability. It relies heavily on Celery for sending out upcoming session reminders via email/SMS.
*   **Analytics Module:** Handles high-throughput, non-blocking telemetry. It tracks anonymized user engagement, mood symptom ratings over time, and generic platform usage.
*   **Resources Module:** Serves reading materials, breathing exercises, and static content metadata regarding mental health.

---

## 4. Infrastructure & Integration Design

### 4.1 Docker Architecture

*   **API Container:** Runs the FastAPI application using `uvicorn` with asynchronous workers. Built using a lightweight Python footprint (`python:3.11-slim`).
*   **Celery Worker Container:** Executes background tasks to keep the API responsive. Shares the same core Python environment as the API but runs the Celery worker entry point (`celery -A app.core.celery_app worker`).
*   **PostgreSQL Container:** Persistent structured data storage.
*   **Redis Container:** Functions as the Celery task broker, a fast key-value cache, and temporal session store for WebSockets.
*   *(Prod Only) Nginx/Traefik Container:* Acts as a reverse proxy, SSL termination point, and load balancer.

### 4.2 Redis & Celery Integration Plan

When a user performs an action that triggers a slow operation (like sending an email, generating an intensive report, or scraping new LLM context data), FastAPI enqueues the task via Redis.
*   **Redis** acts as the message broker. It is also used independently by FastAPI for rate limiting API access and caching repeated heavy queries (e.g., daily schedule fetching).
*   **Celery Workers** continuously monitor the Redis queues, pick up background tasks, execute them asynchronously, and write the state back to PostgreSQL or Redis without blocking the main event loop of FastAPI.

### 4.3 WebSocket Architecture

Real-time capabilities are critical for the Chatbot and potentially live counselor chatting.
*   **Connection Manager (`app/websockets/connection_manager.py`):** An in-memory/Redis-backed dictionary tracking active WebSocket connections.
*   **Authentication:** The WebSocket endpoint first authenticates the initial HTTP handshake using dependency injection (evaluating the user's JWT token).
*   **State Distribution:** Because multiple FastAPI instances will run in production, Redis Pub/Sub will be used. If User A connects to Instance 1 and Counselor B connects to Instance 2, messages are published to a Redis channel and broadcast to the appropriate node.

### 4.4 Chatbot Pipeline Architecture

The AI Mental Health companion must be highly responsive and safe.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    actor Student
    participant WSEndpoint as WebSocket Route
    participant ChatService as Chatbot Module
    participant Redis as Redis (Session Context)
    participant LLM as Langchain / LLM Provider
    participant DB as PostgreSQL

    Student->>WSEndpoint: 1. Send Message via WS
    WSEndpoint->>ChatService: 2. Route payload
    ChatService->>Redis: 3. Fetch past N conversation turns
    ChatService->>LLM: 4. Construct Prompt (System Rules + Context + Message)
    LLM-->>ChatService: 5. Stream response tokens
    ChatService-->>WSEndpoint: 6. Stream tokens to client
    WSEndpoint-->>Student: 7. Real-time UI update
    
    %% Async cleanup
    Note right of ChatService: After response completes
    ChatService-)Redis: 8. Update active conversation cache
    ChatService-)DB: 9. Background save to persistent DB (Async)
```

**Guardrails:** The pipeline includes a lightweight pre-evaluation layer. If a message contains trigger words indicating severe crisis (e.g., self-harm), it bypasses the LLM and instantly replies with emergency contacts and triggers an alert to university counselors via Celery.

### 4.5 Analytics System Design

System metrics must not hinder the performance of core platform features.
*   **Event Emitting:** The platform utilizes FastAPI BackgroundTasks or a fast message queue (Kafka or Redis Streams) to log events rather than writing directly to PostgreSQL.
*   **Aggregation:** A cron-driven Celery task runs nightly to aggregate engagement metrics (e.g., total active users, stress-topic frequency, average session lengths) and compiles a dashboard view for university administrators.
*   **Anonymization:** Data stored in the analytics warehouse strips PII (Personally Identifiable Information) early in the pipeline to comply with student privacy laws (FERPA/HIPAA equivalents).
